  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1235 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nToday\n\nA shortwave trough will eject northeast through the Upper \nMississippi River Valley. The strongest forcing and moisture \nwill be located west of the Mississippi River. While the CAMs \nare showing that the showers will decrease their coverage as \nthey move east toward the Mississippi River, the synoptic \nforcing and 850 mb moisture transport look to be strong enough \nto produce at least some light rainfall totals through the \nmorning and into the mid-afternoon. Due to this, increased the \nrain chances into the 60-100% range during this time frame.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the 60s. Due to the rain and cloud\ncover, the coolest high temperatures (lower 60s) will be west \nof the Mississippi River. Meanwhile, western and central \nWisconsin will see high temperatures in the mid- and upper 60s.\n\nTonight and Sunday \n\nAs the shortwave trough continue to move northeast into a ridge\nof high pressure over the Great Lakes, there will be \nfrontolysis along the surface front and dry southeast winds will\nadvect drier air into the region for tonight and Sunday \nmorning. This should reduce the shower coverage to isolated. \n\nLow temperatures tonight will range from the mid-40s to \nmid-50s. High temperatures on Sunday will be in the mid-60s.\n\nSunday night and Monday night\n\nHigh pressure over the Great Lakes will keep the area dry from \nSunday night into Monday night.\n\nLow temperatures on Sunday night and Monday night will range \nfrom the mid-40s to mid-50s. The coolest temperatures will be in\ncentral and north-central Wisconsin. High temperatures on \nMonday will be around 70 degrees.\n\nTuesday into Wednesday\n\nA northern stream shortwave trough will move through the \nregion. This system looks to be rather progressive, so this may \nresult in lesser rainfall than previously forecast. There is a \n50-80% probability of a half-inch or greater and a 30-65% \nprobability of 1 inch or greater in the NBM.\n\nHigh temperatures on Tuesday and Wednesday will range from \nmid-60s to around 70. Low temperatures on Tuesday night will be \nin the 50s.\n\nThursday into Friday\n\nStill plenty of uncertainty on how far north the remnants of \nPolo will get. A few ensemble members suggest that there might \nbe a PRE (Predecessor Rainfall Event) south of Interstate 90. A \nthird of members suggest that a weakening surface low and its \nassociated warm front may move into the region. Meanwhile, the \nother members keep the area dry. The NBM currently has the \nprobabilities of an inch of rain or greater up to 40%.\n\nHigh temperatures on Thursday and Friday will be in the \nmid-60s. Low temperatures on Thursday night will range from the \nmid-40s to lower 50s.",
